The task is complete. All 3 tests pass and the build succeeds.

## Summary

I implemented the "keep every price watch up to date" behavior using Prefab's **`@Multicast` event handler**, which is the framework's mechanism for fanning one event out to many aggregate instances.

**Changes:**

1. **`PriceWatch.java`** — added a `@Multicast` event handler:
   ```java
   @EventHandler
   @Multicast(queryMethod = "findByProduct", parameters = "product", failWhenEmpty = false)
   public PriceWatch onPriceChanged(PriceChanged event) {
       return new PriceWatch(id, version, email, product, event.price());
   }
   ```
   - The handler runs in the generated `PriceWatchService.onPriceChanged` and the generated `PriceWatchKafkaConsumer` subscribes to topic `prices`.
   - `parameters = "product"` maps the event's `product` field to the query argument, so **only watches of the affected product** are updated; watches of other products are untouched.
   - `failWhenEmpty = false` means a product with no watches produces an empty result set that the generated service returns from cleanly (`if (aggregates.isEmpty()) return;`) — so the event is **not** retried or dead-lettered.

2. **`PriceWatchRepositoryMixin.java`** (new) — the derived query the handler needs:
   ```java
   @RepositoryMixin(PriceWatch.class)
   public interface PriceWatchRepositoryMixin {
       List<PriceWatch> findByProduct(Reference<Product> product);
   }
   ```
   Prefab adds this as a super-interface of the generated `PriceWatchRepository`.

3. **`PriceWatchMulticastIntegrationTest.java`** (new) — an `@IntegrationTest` (in-memory, no Docker) verifying both that two watches of one product both move to the new price while a watch of another product stays put, and that a product with no watches succeeds without error.

The existing `Product.changePrice` (`PUT /products/{id}/price`) already publishes `PriceChanged` on `prices`, and `GET /price-watches/{id}` already exposes `currentPrice` via `@GetById` — no changes needed there.

`mvn verify` passes: the generated `PriceChangedEventConformanceTest` plus my two new tests, 3/3 green, BUILD SUCCESS.

Both tests pass. The implementation is complete and verified.

## Summary

I implemented publishing a Kafka `OrderPlaced` event whenever an order is created through `POST /orders`.

**1. New event record — `src/main/java/com/example/ordering/OrderPlaced.java`**
```java
@Event(topic = "orders")
public record OrderPlaced(
        @PartitioningKey Reference<Order> order,
        String customer,
        BigDecimal total
) {}
```
- Components `order`/`customer`/`total` as specified.
- `@PartitioningKey` on `order` ⇒ events of the same order land on the same Kafka partition (the generated registrar registers the key extractor `event -> event.order().id()`).
- `@Event` defaults to JSON serialization, so the payload is JSON; the platform auto-detects Kafka from the classpath (`prefab-kafka` is already a dependency).

**2. Wired into the create path — `src/main/java/com/example/ordering/Order.java`**
```java
private static final EventPublisher<OrderPlaced> ORDER_PLACED =
        EventPublisher.bind(OrderPlaced.class);

@Create
public Order(@NotNull String customer, @NotNull BigDecimal total) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, customer, total);
    ORDER_PLACED.publish(new OrderPlaced(id, customer, total));
}
```
This is Prefab's recommended synchronous-publish pattern (docs §7.2): the create endpoint still returns `201`, and the event is routed through the Spring event bus to the generated Kafka producer. No `@AsyncCommit` is needed since the task only asks to publish on create, not to defer persistence.

**3. Verification — `src/test/java/com/example/ordering/OrderEventIntegrationTest.java`**
An `@IntegrationTest` (in-memory backend, no Docker) creates an order via the generated `OrderClient` and asserts that exactly one `OrderPlaced` arrives on topic `orders` with the matching `customer`, `total`, and `order().id()` (the partition key material).

**Build/test results:**
- Prefab generated `OrderPlacedEventTypeRegistrar` (registers `orders` → `OrderPlaced`, JSON, key = `order().id()`), an `OrderPlaced` mother/builder, and the JSON round-trip conformance test — agent context confirms `OrderPlaced on topic orders`.
- `mvn test` → `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` — both the generated event conformance test and the new integration test pass; `BUILD SUCCESS`.

No pom changes were needed (`prefab-kafka` was already present), and no generated sources were hand-edited.

The task is complete. The project compiles and all tests pass — both in-memory (default) and against real PostgreSQL via Testcontainers.

## What I did

**1. `src/main/java/com/example/catalog/Book.java`** — the domain aggregate:

```java
@Aggregate
@GetById
public record Book(
        @Id Reference<Book> id,
        @Version long version,
        @NotNull @Size(max = 200) String title,
        @NotNull String author,
        @NotNull String isbn,
        int publicationYear
) {
    @Create
    public Book(@NotNull @Size(max = 200) String title,
                @NotNull String author,
                @NotNull String isbn,
                int publicationYear) {
        this(Reference.create(), 0L, title, author, isbn, publicationYear);
    }
}
```

Prefab's annotation processor generated, at compile time:
- **`BookController`** — `POST /books` (→ `201 Created` with `Location: /books/{id}`) and `GET /books/{id}` (→ `200` with the book, or `404 Not Found` when absent), exactly the required contract.
- **`BookResponse`** — JSON with `id`, `title`, `author`, `isbn`, `publicationYear` (plus the framework-standard `version`).
- **`BookService`**, a Spring Data JDBC **`BookRepository`**, and the **`V1__generated.sql`** Flyway migration creating the `book` table (`title VARCHAR(200)`, `publication_year INTEGER`, etc.) — the PostgreSQL storage the task asks for.

**2. `src/test/java/com/example/catalog/BookIntegrationTest.java`** — an `@IntegrationTest` verifying the full contract: create-then-get round-trip (asserting the `Location` header points to the new id and the returned JSON has all five fields), a `404` for an unknown id, plus two validation checks (title over 200 chars and missing required fields both return `400`).

## Verification
- `mvn clean verify` → **BUILD SUCCESS**, 4/4 tests pass (in-memory backend, the default — no Docker needed).
- Re-ran with `-Dprefab.test.containers=true` → 4/4 pass against a real PostgreSQL Testcontainer, confirming the persistence path.

I left the default in-memory test behavior in place (it's Prefab's recommended, Docker-free path); the production wiring is PostgreSQL via `prefab-postgres`. The response includes the framework-standard `version` field in addition to the five required fields, matching the canonical Prefab quick-start.
